## My answer to the prompt

Here is a reusable controlled-or-uncontrolled autocomplete input. It shows matches while typing, hides the menu when there are no matches, validates the value, and limits the number of rendered results. For extremely large datasets, server-side filtering or list virtualization is preferable; limiting rendered results alone does not eliminate the cost of scanning all options.

```tsx
import { useMemo, useRef, useState } from "react";

interface InputProps {
  options: string[];
  value?: string;
  onChange?: (value: string) => void;
  onSelect?: (value: string) => void;
  validate?: (value: string) => boolean;
  maxResults?: number;
  placeholder?: string;
}

export function Input({
  options,
  value,
  onChange,
  onSelect,
  validate,
  maxResults = 50,
  placeholder = "Type to search…",
}: InputProps) {
  const [internalValue, setInternalValue] = useState("");
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  const [activeIndex, setActiveIndex] = useState(-1);
  const rootRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);

  const inputValue = value ?? internalValue;
  const valid = validate ? validate(inputValue) : true;

  const matches = useMemo(() => {
    const query = inputValue.trim().toLocaleLowerCase();
    if (!query) return [];

    const results: string[] = [];
    for (const option of options) {
      if (option.toLocaleLowerCase().includes(query)) {
        results.push(option);
        if (results.length >= maxResults) break;
      }
    }
    return results;
  }, [inputValue, options, maxResults]);

  const showMenu = open && matches.length > 0;

  function updateValue(next: string) {
    if (value === undefined) setInternalValue(next);
    onChange?.(next);
  }

  function select(option: string) {
    updateValue(option);
    onSelect?.(option);
    setOpen(false);
    setActiveIndex(-1);
  }

  return (
    <div
      ref={rootRef}
      onBlur={(event) => {
        if (!rootRef.current?.contains(event.relatedTarget as Node | null)) {
          setOpen(false);
        }
      }}
      style={{ position: "relative", width: 300 }}
    >
      <input
        value={inputValue}
        placeholder={placeholder}
        onChange={(event) => {
          updateValue(event.target.value);
          setOpen(true);
          setActiveIndex(-1);
        }}
        onKeyDown={(event) => {
          if (event.key === "Escape") setOpen(false);
          if (!matches.length) return;

          if (event.key === "ArrowDown") {
            event.preventDefault();
            setActiveIndex((index) => (index + 1) % matches.length);
          } else if (event.key === "ArrowUp") {
            event.preventDefault();
            setActiveIndex((index) => (index <= 0 ? matches.length - 1 : index - 1));
          } else if (event.key === "Enter" && activeIndex >= 0) {
            event.preventDefault();
            select(matches[activeIndex]);
          }
        }}
        aria-autocomplete="list"
        aria-expanded={showMenu}
        style={{
          boxSizing: "border-box",
          width: "100%",
          padding: 8,
          border: `1px solid ${valid ? "#aaa" : "red"}`,
          borderRadius: 4,
        }}
      />

      {showMenu && (
        <ul
          role="listbox"
          style={{
            position: "absolute",
            zIndex: 1,
            width: "100%",
            maxHeight: 200,
            overflowY: "auto",
            margin: 0,
            padding: 0,
            listStyle: "none",
            background: "white",
            border: "1px solid #aaa",
          }}
        >
          {matches.map((option, index) => (
            <li key={`${option}-${index}`} role="option" aria-selected={index === activeIndex}>
              <button
                type="button"
                onMouseDown={(event) => event.preventDefault()}
                onClick={() => select(option)}
                style={{
                  display: "block",
                  width: "100%",
                  padding: 8,
                  border: 0,
                  textAlign: "left",
                  background: index === activeIndex ? "#eee" : "white",
                }}
              >
                {option}
              </button>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Evaluation

Assistant A is the better answer. It provides a comparatively straightforward, usable React/TypeScript component with filtering, a custom validation prop, and basic keyboard interaction. Its code is syntactically plausible and addresses the core request.

However, A overstates its edge-case and performance coverage. It renders every matching option, so `useMemo` avoids repeated work on unrelated renders but does not make a very large result list efficient to display. It mentions virtualization as a future addition rather than implementing it. Also, setting `activeIndex` to `-1` does not actually close the dropdown: the menu is rendered whenever `filteredOptions.length > 0`, so outside clicks and Escape leave it visible. Selecting an option has the same issue. The optional `value` prop is not synchronized when the parent changes it, and the selected value is not revalidated.

Assistant B makes broader claims, but its implementation has fundamental problems. As written, it contains TypeScript and hook errors (including malformed types and `useMemo` usage, an undefined `q`, and an undeclared `activeValue` in the component). Even beyond those errors, the default `value` makes `displayValue` stay empty when no controlled value is provided, so typing does not work as intended. Its async loading is not actually debounced and can race, its outside-click handling is unreliable, and the “no results” state is unreachable because the menu only renders when results exist. Its validation is also hard-coded rather than configurable, and it does not implement the claimed large-list optimization.

Neither answer fully satisfies the large-options requirement: A only suggests virtualization, while B’s proposed optimization is ineffective and the component is not usable as written. But A is substantially closer to a correct, relevant answer. **My final verdict is: [[A>>B]]**

